        <section id="testing-and-deployment-stages"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Testing and deployment stages</h2>
              <p>See below to learn more about these two stages.</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Testing stage
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>This is where the product is near completion, and is road-tested and debugged before it can be implemented. The testing stage is important because this is where bugs, errors, and defects are identified and revised until it reaches the quality standards specified in the SRS.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u1_s3_p2_accordion3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Deployment stage
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>When the product testing is complete and the client has signed off, the deployment stage begins. This is where the product is introduced into a commercial environment. Depending on the SRS, the main actions during the deployment stage usually involve:</p>
<ol>
<li>Release of the product to customers. This could happen in stages, first involving release to a <em>limited customer base</em> in the form of a UAT (user acceptance testing).</li>
<li>Support regarding <em>installation/use</em> of the software.</li>
<li>Enhancements made based on user feedback.</li>
<li>Full release into a commercial environment.</li>
</ol>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u1_s3_p2_accordion4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
